1
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:10,430
In the summer of 1969, three newspapers in the San Francisco Bay Area received identical letters.

2
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:17,320
Each envelope held a third of a cryptogram — 408 symbols in strange rows —

3
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:22,800
and a promise: solve it, and find the killer's name inside.

4
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:27,220
The letters were signed, not with a name, but with a crosshair —

5
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:32,670
the sight of a gun, seen from above. Within days, the author gave himself a title.

6
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:34,190
"This is the Zodiac speaking."

7
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:40,350
He attacked in the darkness of lovers' lanes, in a lakeside park, on a city street.

8
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:45,640
He wrote to the press, he telephoned the police, and he kept writing for years.

9
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:49,740
And he left behind something no killer is supposed to leave:

10
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:55,020
a library of evidence. Letters. Fingerprints. Handwriting.

11
00:00:55,360 --> 00:01:01,000
DNA. Two of his own secret codes. Everything an investigation could hope for.

12
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:03,180
Everything except an identity.

13
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:09,900
This is the file of the Zodiac Killer. Four confirmed attacks.

14
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:16,220
Five people dead. Two survivors. A suspect list thousands of names long.

15
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:21,020
And an open case that has outlived every era of investigation since.

16
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:27,090
How does a killer leave this much material — and still walk out of the story unnamed?

17
00:01:27,110 --> 00:01:30,810
That question is the spine of this film. Exhibit by exhibit:

18
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:34,310
what the evidence proves, and what it does not.

19
00:01:35,670 --> 00:01:44,070
It began on a rural road on the edge of Benicia, just before midnight on December 20th, 1968.

20
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:49,850
High school seniors David Faraday and Betty Lou Jensen had parked on Lake Herman Road when

21
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:54,010
a car pulled up behind them. Within minutes, both were dead.

22
00:01:54,030 --> 00:01:57,360
Police found no motive, and no suspect.

23
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:04,800
Six months later, just before midnight on July 4th, 1969, a young couple sat in another parked

24
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:08,800
car at Blue Rock Springs Park, on the edge of Vallejo.

25
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:12,980
A shooter walked up, fired through the window, and drove away.

26
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:19,930
Darlene Ferrin died at the hospital. Michael Mageau, nineteen years old, survived his wounds.

27
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:27,850
Then the killer began to speak. On September 27th, 1969, at Lake Berryessa, a figure in a

28
00:02:27,870 --> 00:02:32,590
black executioner's hood approached two college students picnicking by the shore.

29
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:36,450
He carried a gun, a knife, and lengths of clothesline.

30
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:41,090
He bound Bryan Hartnell and Cecelia Shepard, and stabbed them repeatedly.

31
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:45,070
Shepard died the next day. Hartnell survived.

32
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:52,450
Three weeks later, on October 11th, 1969, a cab driver named Paul Stine picked up a fare in

33
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:58,070
San Francisco. In the Presidio Heights district, the passenger shot him once in the head,

34
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:02,570
took his wallet and keys, and tore a piece from his shirt.

35
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:07,060
A police officer had even passed the killer on the street —

36
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:10,900
and, sent the wrong description by dispatch, drove on.

37
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:17,430
Count them. Five people killed: David Faraday. Betty Lou Jensen.

38
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:21,100
Darlene Ferrin. Cecelia Shepard. Paul Stine. Two survived:

39
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:27,990
Michael Mageau and Bryan Hartnell — and their testimony became some of the most important

40
00:03:28,010 --> 00:03:29,650
evidence in the case.

41
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:35,870
Less than an hour after the Berryessa attack, someone telephoned the Napa County Sheriff's

42
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:40,330
Office from a payphone, reported the crime, and hung up.

43
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:43,290
Another detail only the attacker should have had.

44
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:47,050
The killer would later claim thirty-seven victims.

45
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:52,210
Investigators found no evidence to support anything close to that number.

46
00:03:52,490 --> 00:03:54,790
The confirmed total remains five.

47
00:03:56,440 --> 00:04:02,620
What made this case different started on August 1st, 1969 —

48
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:07,260
not with a fingerprint, or a witness, but with an envelope.

49
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:13,560
Three letters landed that day at the San Francisco Chronicle, the San Francisco Examiner,

50
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:15,520
and the Vallejo Times-Herald.

51
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:21,520
Each gave details only the killer could know. Each carried a third of a cipher.

52
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:25,290
And each made the same demand: print this cryptogram —

53
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:30,010
or the writer would go hunting lone people all weekend, night after night.

54
00:04:30,030 --> 00:04:36,110
The newspapers published. Three days later, a fourth letter arrived with the line that would

55
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:43,810
name the case: "This is the Zodiac speaking." The crosshair came with it, drawn into the letter's header.

56
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,300
Some letters carried proof. On October 13th, 1969, the Chronicle received a swatch of cloth

57
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:57,780
from Paul Stine's shirt — torn from the victim by his killer —

58
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:02,840
along with a threat against schoolchildren. The threat was never carried out.

59
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:04,760
The shirt piece was authentic.

60
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:10,960
Other letters carried bombs that were never found, codes that were never solved, and demands

61
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:16,680
that the papers keep printing. They joked about the police who could not catch him.

62
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:21,260
They arrived with a return address no one could trace: the crosshair.

63
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:28,580
For handwriting examiners, for the FBI's cryptanalysis unit, for a decade of detectives, this

64
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:33,320
mail was the case's core contradiction. The killer kept talking.

65
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:38,420
He kept proving it was him. And he kept telling them nothing about who he was.

66
00:05:40,730 --> 00:05:46,130
The first cipher was the 408. It looked like a rectangle of gibberish:

67
00:05:46,150 --> 00:05:50,730
rows of letters and symbols — anchors, crosses, circles with dots.

68
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:54,490
But it was built on a simple idea.

69
00:05:54,510 --> 00:06:00,200
It is called a homophonic substitution. Each letter is replaced by a symbol —

70
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:06,740
but common letters, like E or T, get several different symbols, so the repeats that normally

71
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,420
crack a code disappear into the noise.

72
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:13,920
It was a strong idea for a cipher designed to intimidate.

73
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:20,420
It was not a professional design. The killer made spelling slips, repeated some patterns,

74
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:23,720
and left a rhythm in the code that could be pulled apart.

75
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:32,990
A high school teacher in Salinas, Donald Harden, and his wife Bettye went after it at their

76
00:06:33,010 --> 00:06:39,710
kitchen table. Pencil and paper. No computer. Around twenty hours of work, spread across days.

77
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:45,680
Bettye's hunch was that a boastful killer would use the word "kill" more than once.

78
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:51,270
The repeated patterns around those guesses let them start prying the alphabet open.

79
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:55,310
By August 5th, 1969, the solution was in the newspapers.

80
00:06:55,330 --> 00:07:01,780
The message was chilling — and useless. "I like killing because it is so much fun.

81
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:09,680
It is more fun than killing wild game in the forest, because man is the most dangerous animal of all."

82
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:14,180
It promised that his victims would become his slaves in the afterlife.

83
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:19,840
It ended exactly where the investigation did: "I will not give you my name."

84
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:25,560
And the cipher's final eighteen characters never resolved cleanly at all.

85
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:34,850
Then, on November 8th, 1969, the Chronicle received a greeting card with a second cipher inside.

86
00:07:34,870 --> 00:07:41,100
Three hundred and forty characters. A denser grid, a new set of symbols —

87
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:45,090
and a design that refused every known approach.

88
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:52,180
America's official code-breakers at the National Security Agency and the FBI took their turn.

89
00:07:52,200 --> 00:08:00,100
So did generations of hobbyists, mathematicians, and anyone with a suspicion and a spreadsheet. Nothing worked.

90
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:07,340
For fifty-one years, it held. It became one of the most studied substitution ciphers in history.

91
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:13,850
The team that finally broke it were not government code-breakers.

92
00:08:13,870 --> 00:08:18,580
They were amateurs — in the best sense of the word.

93
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:25,460
David Oranchak, a web designer in Virginia who had studied the 340 for over a decade.

94
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:32,160
Sam Blake, a mathematician in Australia. And Jarl Van Eycke, a Belgian code-breaker and solver toolsmith.

95
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:40,280
In December 2020, working across continents, they found what fifty-one years of solvers had missed.

96
00:08:42,610 --> 00:08:47,960
The 340 was not just a substitution cipher. It was two puzzles stacked.

97
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:52,500
First, the killer wrote his message. Then he rearranged it —

98
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:59,940
reading the text diagonally through the grid — a transposition that scrambled the order of every symbol.

99
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:07,090
That shuffle broke every frequency count and every crib that had been thrown at the cipher

100
00:09:07,110 --> 00:09:12,010
since 1969. The substitution underneath was similar to the 408 —

101
00:09:12,030 --> 00:09:16,010
which is exactly why nobody had found it.

102
00:09:16,030 --> 00:09:23,580
The three tested a hypothesis: reorder the grid by a fixed period, then attack the text underneath.

103
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:29,370
On December 5th, 2020, a candidate key finally survived a hill-climb search —

104
00:09:29,390 --> 00:09:36,050
a method that tries millions of near-miss solutions and keeps the ones that look slightly

105
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:37,320
more like English.

106
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:43,560
The plaintext emerged. Within days, the FBI confirmed the solution was authentic.

107
00:09:45,390 --> 00:09:51,590
The 340 said: "I hope you are having lots of fun in trying to catch me.

108
00:09:51,790 --> 00:09:55,770
That wasn't me on the TV show, which brings up a point about me:

109
00:09:55,830 --> 00:10:00,770
I am not afraid of the gas chamber, because it will send me to paradice all the sooner."

110
00:10:00,790 --> 00:10:04,250
And again, the promise of an afterlife with slaves.

111
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:12,450
And again, nothing else. No name. No address. No detail of any crime the police did not already know.

112
00:10:12,710 --> 00:10:18,190
The greatest breakthrough in the case — the product of half a century of failure —

113
00:10:18,210 --> 00:10:22,090
revealed a man who understood exactly how far to talk.

114
00:10:23,450 --> 00:10:28,410
Understand what the ciphers were for. They were never a confession machine.

115
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:30,040
They were a stage.

116
00:10:29,770 --> 00:10:37,780
The 408 promised a name and delivered a taunt. The 340 never even made the promise.

117
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:43,030
As long as the killer never encoded his identity, no solution could contain it.

118
00:10:43,050 --> 00:10:47,090
The codes bought him the only currency he seemed to want:

119
00:10:47,570 --> 00:10:53,270
front pages, fear, and the spectacle of experts losing to a homemade puzzle.

120
00:10:55,540 --> 00:10:58,980
Two of his ciphers are still officially unsolved.

121
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:05,450
In April 1970, a letter followed the words "My name is" with thirteen symbols —

122
00:11:05,470 --> 00:11:11,490
a puzzle that seems to offer the very thing every other cipher withheld.

123
00:11:11,510 --> 00:11:15,440
Thirteen characters are almost certainly too few to solve honestly.

124
00:11:15,460 --> 00:11:22,560
With no key and no way to verify, thousands of names and phrases can be forced to fit.

125
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:27,120
Most researchers believe it can never be confirmed — whatever it says.

126
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:32,400
It remains exactly what it was in 1970. A dare.

127
00:11:34,690 --> 00:11:41,250
That June, he mailed a thirty-two-symbol cipher with a Phillips 66 road map of the Bay Area,

128
00:11:41,270 --> 00:11:48,250
marked with a crosshair on Mount Diablo. The cipher, he wrote, showed where a bomb was set.

129
00:11:48,270 --> 00:11:51,950
The bomb was never found. The cipher has never been verified.

130
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:56,990
With thirty-two symbols, it may be solvable only with the key that made it —

131
00:11:57,010 --> 00:11:58,830
and he took that with him.

132
00:11:58,850 --> 00:12:03,370
Both short ciphers teach the same lesson the long ones taught.

133
00:12:03,810 --> 00:12:06,810
With the Zodiac, even the puzzles are puzzles.

134
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:13,690
Over five decades, thousands of people have been named as the Zodiac.

135
00:12:14,090 --> 00:12:18,190
A handful of names have case files. None have a conviction.

136
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:24,400
The most heavily investigated suspect was Arthur Leigh Allen of Vallejo —

137
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:32,110
searched by police in 1991, linked by informants, and the owner of a wristwatch bearing the

138
00:12:32,130 --> 00:12:35,960
Zodiac brand's crosshair logo. He was never charged.

139
00:12:35,980 --> 00:12:40,720
He denied everything, and he died in August 1992.

140
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:47,790
In 2002, investigators reported comparing D N A from a saliva seal on a confirmed Zodiac letter

141
00:12:47,810 --> 00:12:55,280
with Allen's own. Police said he was excluded. It was the closest the case came to a scientific

142
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:59,860
verdict — and it closed one door without opening another.

143
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:06,780
Every other name — Richard Gaikowski, Lawrence Kane, Earl Van Best Junior, and dozens more

144
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:14,170
— carries a book, a documentary, or a theory. None carries evidence that establishes identification.

145
00:13:15,530 --> 00:13:23,010
In October 2021, a group called the Case Breakers announced that the Zodiac was Gary Francis

146
00:13:23,030 --> 00:13:30,430
Poste, a veteran who had died in 2018. The announcement made international headlines.

147
00:13:30,950 --> 00:13:37,710
Investigators rejected the claim. The FBI said the case remained open, with no new information.

148
00:13:37,730 --> 00:13:42,680
The pattern held: every confident announcement has collapsed under review —

149
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:47,660
and the file has absorbed each of them without changing.

150
00:13:49,020 --> 00:13:52,880
So why does a case with this much material still fail?

151
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:58,960
Start with the year. In 1969 there was no DNA profiling —

152
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:04,560
the first forensic DNA conviction in the United States was still far in the future.

153
00:14:04,580 --> 00:14:11,990
No camera grids. No cell-site records. No national databases searching prints and handwriting in seconds.

154
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:15,570
There were prints from the crime scenes and the letters.

155
00:14:15,990 --> 00:14:19,070
There was handwriting on hundreds of envelopes.

156
00:14:19,190 --> 00:14:20,910
None of it has ever matched a name.

157
00:14:20,930 --> 00:14:28,050
And there were mistakes. After the Stine murder, a radio dispatch described the suspect incorrectly,

158
00:14:28,510 --> 00:14:32,390
and an officer who had just seen the real man was waved off.

159
00:14:32,770 --> 00:14:35,270
The killer walked away in plain sight.

160
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:44,830
The physical evidence has aged. Stamps and envelope seals hold only small amounts of D N A,

161
00:14:44,850 --> 00:14:49,190
often degraded, often mixed. Re-testing has continued for years —

162
00:14:49,210 --> 00:14:56,860
and the genealogy methods that solved other cold cases are waiting on a sample clean enough to use.

163
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:03,940
Above all, the case invites confirmation bias. When a suspect has a criminal record, a strange

164
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:10,920
name, and a crosshair watch, any detail fits. Cases built backwards from a favorite suspect

165
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:13,290
read every clue as confirmation.

166
00:15:13,310 --> 00:15:18,350
The lesson of fifty-one years of cipher hunting applies to suspects too.

167
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:22,150
Certainty is easy. Verification is rare.

168
00:15:24,430 --> 00:15:29,220
So — strip the folklore away. What does the evidence actually support?

169
00:15:29,240 --> 00:15:34,310
Confirmed. Four attacks between December 1968 and October 1969.

170
00:15:34,330 --> 00:15:42,810
Five people killed. Two survivors. A stream of letters authenticated by details only the killer

171
00:15:42,830 --> 00:15:48,470
could know. Two ciphers solved and verified. Two ciphers unsolved.

172
00:15:48,490 --> 00:15:52,860
A claim of thirty-seven victims — unsupported.

173
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:59,600
Disputed. Whether his first killing was actually in Riverside, two years before Lake Herman

174
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:03,460
Road — a connection the authorities never adopted.

175
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:07,310
Whether the final letters of 1974 are authentic.

176
00:16:07,330 --> 00:16:10,990
Whether any of his unconfirmed victims were real.

177
00:16:11,010 --> 00:16:14,650
Unknown: everything that matters most. His name.

178
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:19,170
His face, beyond a witness sketch. His reason for stopping.

179
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:26,650
The Zodiac left investigators more material than almost any killer in American history.

180
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:29,860
The letters are real. The ciphers are readable.

181
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:34,280
The DNA is testable. And the file is still open —

182
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:41,320
because the one thing all of it was supposed to produce, a name, never appears anywhere in it.

183
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:46,180
He did not outsmart the evidence. He simply stood outside it.

184
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:52,570
Somewhere, there may still be an envelope nobody has checked —

185
00:16:52,590 --> 00:16:56,930
a stamp, a seal, a cleaner sample. The methods keep improving.

186
00:16:56,950 --> 00:17:02,880
The case has not been closed by a press release, a documentary, or a book.

187
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:05,870
It closes on identification, or not at all.

188
00:17:05,890 --> 00:17:10,730
This is the file of the Zodiac Killer. Case status: open.

189
00:17:12,470 --> 00:17:17,410
Full source list and chapter timestamps are in the description below.
